From ac44f4d8b1d0bb2fc1ffa3dc79249575c82a7d1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vladyslav Horbachov <0965142838gvs@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 11:23:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] refactor(modules): split Integrations into Google and
 Salesforce on a shared SDK

Kernel/DigitalBrain.Sdk is the reusable module infrastructure on Core:

- Sdk/Mcp: McpToolClient<TConnection> owns one authenticated Streamable HTTP
  session per owner (gate, lifetime, capacity, reset on failure), the bearer
  handler (per-request token, redirect refusal, optional response budget),
  the per-session catalog check, tool lookup, result normalization and the
  single read-only refresh-and-retry after a 401. IMcpCredentials<TConnection>
  is the provider seam; McpToolPolicy carries the read/write classification
  and the catalog contract as data instead of hard-coded tool names.
- Sdk/Http: IHttpSurface lets a module map its browser callbacks; the kernel
  calls UseModuleHttpSurfaces() once, before authentication and the Basic
  gate, and no longer names any provider.

Modules/Google and Modules/Salesforce are the standard triple. Contracts hold
IGmail and ISalesforce (owner-less overloads gone); the implementations keep
their OAuth, pending-login and confirmation policy and drop their private
session and bearer code; fakes live under Testing/. Each Aspire.Hosting
projection declares the module's own parameters (WithGmail, WithHostedMcp),
derives PublicOrigin from the kernel's http endpoint, and declares nothing in
fakes mode. Configuration keys move to the module roots
(DigitalBrain:Google:Gmail:OAuth, DigitalBrain:Salesforce:OAuth|Mcp); the four
parameter names and the /integrations/* login URLs are unchanged.

The web search facade moves into SmartPrompt, its only consumer. The orphaned
fake MCP server project and the tests that expected fake-*-mcp resources are
deleted; the Aspire conformance tests now check the module-owned parameters.

